  "text": "```verilog\nmodule axis_register #\n(\n    parameter DATA_WIDTH = 8,\n    parameter KEEP_ENABLE = (DATA_WIDTH>8),\n    parameter KEEP_WIDTH = ((DATA_WIDTH+7)/8),\n    parameter LAST_ENABLE = 1,\n    parameter ID_ENABLE = 0,\n    parameter ID_WIDTH = 8,\n    parameter DEST_ENABLE = 0,\n    parameter DEST_WIDTH = 8,\n    parameter USER_ENABLE = 1,\n    parameter USER_WIDTH = 1,\n    parameter REG_TYPE = 2\n)\n(\n    input  wire                   clk,\n    input  wire                   rst,\n    /*\n     * AXI Stream input\n     */\n    input  wire [DATA_WIDTH-1:0]  s_axis_tdata,\n    input  wire [KEEP_WIDTH-1:0]  s_axis_tkeep,\n    input  wire                   s_axis_tvalid,\n    output wire                   s_axis_tready,\n    input  wire                   s_axis_tlast,\n    input  wire [ID_WIDTH-1:0]    s_axis_tid,\n    input  wire [DEST_WIDTH-1:0]  s_axis_tdest,\n    input  wire [USER_WIDTH-1:0]  s_axis_tuser,\n    /*\n     * AXI Stream output\n     */\n    output wire [DATA_WIDTH-1:0]  m_axis_tdata,\n    output wire [KEEP_WIDTH-1:0]  m_axis_tkeep,\n    output wire                   m_axis_tvalid,\n    input  wire                   m_axis_tready,\n    output wire                   m_axis_tlast,\n    output wire [ID_WIDTH-1:0]    m_axis_tid,\n    output wire [DEST_WIDTH-1:0]  m_axis_tdest,\n    output wire [USER_WIDTH-1:0]  m_axis_tuser\n);\n\n    // Internal state registers\n    reg [DATA_WIDTH-1:0] reg_data;\n    reg [KEEP_WIDTH-1:0] reg_keep;\n    reg reg_last;\n    reg [ID_WIDTH-1:0] reg_id;\n    reg [DEST_WIDTH-1:0] reg_dest;\n    reg [USER_WIDTH-1:0] reg_user;\n    reg reg_valid;\n\n    // Control signals\n    wire wire_mode = (REG_TYPE == 0); // Wire\n    wire buffer_mode = (REG_TYPE == 1); // Buffer\n    wire skid_mode = (REG_TYPE == 2); // Skid Buffer\n\n    // Logic to determine if we should accept new data\n    // In skid buffer mode, we always accept if valid and ready, regardless of output ready\n    // In wire/buffer mode, we only accept if output is ready (unless we are just holding state)\n    // However, standard AXI-Stream register behavior:\n    // Wire: Pass through immediately.\n    // Buffer: Store until ready, then pass.\n    // Skid: Store one beat, then pass immediately next cycle regardless of ready (skid).\n    \n    // Determine if we can write to the register\n    wire can_write = (s_axis_tvalid && s_axis_tready);\n    \n    // For Skid Buffer, we write on every valid input, even if output is not ready yet (to clear the input)\n    // But we only output if m_axis_tready is high.\n    // Actually, standard skid buffer logic:\n    // If input valid and ready, store data.\n    // If output ready, output stored data.\n    // The \"skid\" happens because the output might be ready in the next cycle even if input was ready this cycle.\n    \n    // Let's refine the logic based on REG_TYPE:\n    // 0 (Wire): s_axis_tready = m_axis_tready. Data passes through. No storage needed really, but we use registers to handle the handshake.\n    // 1 (Buffer): Store data. s_axis_tready = m_axis_tready. Only output when ready.\n    // 2 (Skid): Store data. s_axis_tready = 1'b1 (always ready to accept input). Output when m_axis_tready.\n    \n    assign s_axis_tready = \n        (wire_mode) ? m_axis_tready : \n        (buffer_mode) ? m_axis_tready : \n        (skid_mode) ? 1'b1 : 1'b0;\n\n    // Update logic\n    always @(posedge clk or posedge rst) begin\n        if (rst) begin\n            reg_data <= 0;\n            reg_keep <= 0;\n            reg_last <= 0;\n            reg_id <= 0;\n            reg_dest <= 0;\n            reg_user <= 0;\n            reg_valid <= 0;\n        end else begin\n            // Determine if we update the register\n            wire update_reg = (s_axis_tvalid && s_axis_tready);\n            \n            if (update_reg) begin\n                reg_data <= s_axis_tdata;\n                reg_keep <= s_axis_tkeep;\n                reg_last <= s_axis_tlast;\n                reg_id <= s_axis_tid;\n                reg_dest <= s_axis_tdest;\n                reg_user <= s_axis_tuser;\n                reg_valid <= 1'b1;\n            end else begin\n                // For Wire mode, if we can't write (because output not ready), we don't store, \n                // but we must ensure the output reflects the input if it was a wire.\n                // However, the register holds the last valid value.\n                // If it's a wire, and input is valid but output not ready, we hold the previous value?\n                // No, a wire should reflect the input. If input is valid and output not ready, \n                // the wire effectively stalls. The register holds the last valid data.\n                // If input becomes invalid, the register should ideally clear or hold?\n                // Standard behavior: The register holds the last valid transaction.\n                // If s_axis_tvalid is 0, we don't update.\n                // If s_axis_tvalid is 1 and s_axis_tready is 0, we don't update (we are stalled).\n                // So reg_valid remains 1 if we had a valid transaction previously.\n                // Wait, if it's a wire, and input stops, does the output stop? Yes.\n                // If input stops (valid=0), reg_valid should become 0?\n                // Let's assume the register holds the last valid packet.\n                // If new packet arrives and is accepted, update.\n                // If no new packet, keep old.\n                // But if s_axis_tvalid goes low, we should probably clear reg_valid for a wire/buffer \n                // unless we are in a skid buffer scenario where we might have a pending output.\n                \n                // Refined Logic for Wire/Buffer:\n                // If s_axis_tvalid is 0, we don't have new data. \n                // If we are in Wire mode, we should ideally not hold data if input is gone?\n                // Actually, the register is the storage. If input is valid, we store.\n                // If input is invalid, we don't store.\n                // The output is valid if reg_valid is 1 AND m_axis_tready is 1.\n                \n                // Special case for Skid Buffer:\n                // We always accept input (s_axis_tready = 1).\n                // We update the register on every valid input.\n                // We output if m_axis_tready is 1.\n                // If m_axis_tready is 0, we hold the data in the register for the next cycle.\n                \n                // Special case for Wire/Buffer:\n                // We only accept input if m_axis_tready is 1.\n                // We update the register only when we accept.\n                // If we don't accept (m_axis_tready=0), we hold the register value.\n                // If s_axis_tvalid becomes 0, we don't update, so reg_valid stays 1?\n                // This means if input stops, output continues with last data until m_axis_tready goes low?\n                // That is incorrect for a simple register. A register should not generate data if there is no input.\n                // Correction: reg_valid should be updated based on s_axis_tvalid.\n                // If s_axis_tvalid is 0, reg_valid becomes 0.\n                // If s_axis_tvalid is 1 and ready, reg_valid becomes 1.\n                \n                // Let's re-evaluate \"Wire\" behavior.\n                // A wire passes data. If input is valid, output is valid.\n                // If input is invalid, output is invalid.\n                // So reg_valid should follow s_axis_tvalid (if accepted).\n                \n                // Logic:\n                // If (s_axis_tvalid && s_axis_tready) -> Update register, reg_valid = 1.\n                // Else if (!s_axis_tvalid) -> reg_valid = 0.\n                // Else (s_axis_tvalid && !s_axis_tready) -> Hold register, reg_valid = 1 (stall).\n                \n                // Wait, if it's a wire, and input is valid but output not ready, we stall.\n                // If input becomes invalid, we must clear the output.\n                // So:\n                // if (s_axis_tvalid && s_axis_tready) begin\n                //    update registers\n                //    reg_valid = 1\n                // end else if (!s_axis_tvalid) begin\n                //    reg_valid = 0\n                // end else begin\n                //    // s_axis_tvalid is 1, but s_axis_tready is 0\n                //    // We are stalled. Keep current register value and valid flag.\n                // end\n                \n                // This logic works for Buffer and Skid too, with the exception of Skid's ready signal.\n                // For Skid, s_axis_tready is always 1. So we always update if s_axis_tvalid is 1.\n                // And we output if m_axis_tready is 1.\n                \n                // One nuance: If s_axis_tvalid is 0, we set reg_valid = 0.\n                // If s_axis_tvalid is 1 and s_axis_tready is 0 (Buffer mode), we keep reg_valid = 1.\n                // If s_axis_tvalid is 1 and s_axis_tready is 1, we update and set reg_valid = 1.\n                \n                // Is there a case where we want to clear the register even if valid?\n                // Only if the input stream explicitly says \"no data\" (valid=0).\n                \n                // Let's implement the update logic carefully.\n                \n                if (s_axis_tvalid) begin\n                    if (s_axis_tready) begin\n                        // Accepted\n                        reg_data <= s_axis_tdata;\n                        reg_keep <= s_axis_tkeep;\n                        reg_last <= s_axis_tlast;\n                        reg_id <= s_axis_tid;\n                        reg_dest <= s_axis_tdest;\n                        reg_user <= s_axis_tuser;\n                        reg_valid <= 1'b1;\n                    end else begin\n                        // Stalled (Input valid, Output not ready)\n                        // Keep data, keep valid\n                        // Do nothing to registers\n                    end\n                end else begin\n                    // Input invalid\n                    reg_valid <= 1'b0;\n                    // Data doesn't matter anymore\n                end\n            end\n        end\n    end\n\n    // Output logic\n    always @(*) begin\n        m_axis_tdata = reg_data;\n        m_axis_tkeep = reg_keep;\n        m_axis_tvalid = (reg_valid && m_axis_tready);\n        m_axis_tlast = reg_last;\n        m_axis_tid = reg_id;\n        m_axis_tdest = reg_dest;\n        m_axis_tuser = reg_user;\n    end\n\nendmodule\n```<|im_end|>\n<|endoftext|>",
